.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.TH PKGADD 8 "" "imai #VERSION#" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pkgadd \- install a CRUX software package
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkgadd
.RI [ options ] " file"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pkgadd
installs a package archive and records its name, version and file inventory
in the package database. Root privileges are required.
Package filenames have the form
.IR name # version - release .pkg.tar. compression ,
where compression is gz, bz2, xz, lz or zst.
Dependencies are not resolved automatically.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-upgrade
Replace an installed package of the same name. Without this option,
installing an already installed package is an error.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite conflicting files and remove their ownership from other package
records. Use with care; this can damage other installed packages.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-root " " path
Use an alternative installation root and its package database instead of
.IR / .
This is for another system's root filesystem, not for relocating a package
from /usr to /usr/local. The archive pathname remains relative to the caller.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " file
Read an alternative configuration file. This explicit pathname is interpreted
in the caller's filesystem, not relative to the installation root.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the invoked command name, imai identity and version, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The default configuration is
.I /etc/pkgadd.conf
under the selected installation root. Each nonempty rule has exactly three
whitespace-separated tokens:
.PP
.RS
.I event pattern action
.RE
.PP
Events are case-sensitive
.B INSTALL
and
.BR UPGRADE ;
actions are
.B YES
and
.BR NO .
Patterns are POSIX extended regular expressions matched against the original
archive member names, normally without an initial slash.
.B INSTALL
rules apply to all installations;
.B INSTALL NO
excludes a member from installation and the new inventory.
.B UPGRADE
rules control replacement during upgrades. The default is YES for both events.
The last matching rule of the relevant event takes precedence.
.PP
.nf
UPGRADE ^etc/.*$ NO
UPGRADE ^var/log/.*$ NO
UPGRADE ^etc/X11/.*$ YES
.fi
.PP
This preserves existing files below etc and var/log except below etc/X11.
An incoming file that must not replace an existing file is routed to
.I /var/lib/pkg/rejected/
under the selected root for manual examination with
.BR rejmerge (8).
Identical rejected copies may be discarded.
.PP
A comment must begin with # in the first column. Inline comments and extra
tokens are not accepted. Lines reaching the parser's 1024-byte limit are
rejected. Regular expressions are compiled when considered, so an invalid
pattern superseded by a higher-priority match need not be diagnosed.
Missing or unopenable configuration files are silently ignored, as in
pkgutils. A stream read error aborts before mutation rather than using a
partially read configuration.
.SH SAFETY AND COMPATIBILITY
Packages are unsigned local input and are not authenticated by pkgadd.
Only install packages from publishers trusted with system-wide installation
authority. Archive checks do not make installed software safe.
.PP
imai validates and retains the decoded archive before extraction, requiring
temporary disk space. Absolute member names and names containing parent
traversal components are rejected, including hardlink targets. Harmless dot
components and repeated separators are accepted; original inventory spellings
remain significant for ownership and configuration rules.
Package names, versions and member names containing a newline are rejected
before mutation because the plain-text database cannot represent them without
changing record boundaries.
.PP
Privileged lookups use Linux root-scoped resolution with magic links refused;
required protections have no weaker fallback. Symlink contents may be absolute
or contain parent components, with lookups scoped to the selected root.
Authority over selected objects can persist after another process relocates
them outside that root. Continuous structural containment is not promised.
An alternative root is not a sandbox. The host
.I /sbin/ldconfig
helper may be invoked for the selected root when etc/ld.so.conf exists.
.PP
Updates are nontransactional. Once mutation starts, catchable HUP, INT, QUIT
and TERM requests are deferred until best-effort completion and database
finalization, then reported. This is not rollback and does not protect
against SIGKILL, crashes or power loss.
.PP
Helper setup, execution or wait failures are reported as failures, but do not
skip best-effort database finalization after mutation. This differs from
pkgutils failure paths that can leave the original database after changing files.
A helper that runs and exits nonzero retains the legacy ignored-exit policy.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero retains pkgutils compatibility and is not proof that every file was
installed successfully. In particular, an upgrade may report extraction errors
and still finalize its database with status zero. A fresh extraction failure
attempts cleanup and reports failure. Inspect diagnostics and verify important
files. Argument, preflight and database errors normally return nonzero.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pkgadd.conf
Default installation rules.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/db
Plain-text package database; the database directory is locked during access.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/db.backup
Previous database snapshot.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/db.incomplete_transaction
Intermediate database output; a file left after failure may be partial.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/rejected/
Incoming files preserved for manual merging.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Complete package-corpus mutation compatibility is not established. Runtime
tests have used Linux 6.18.62 on x86-64; minimum-kernel runtime compatibility
has not been verified. Required lookup protections are checked at runtime.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkgrm (8),
.BR pkginfo (8),
.BR pkgmk (8),
.BR rejmerge (8)
